Estimate a powerlifting lift-profile stimulus coefficient for INOL.

The profile is structured: technique options for the lift, volume tolerance,
and optional notes. Output only the coefficient, confidence and reasoning.
Range is 1.0 to 2.0. Baseline is 1.0:
- competition-standard depth/ROM
- conventional grip/stance for the lift
- no notable mechanical advantage or disadvantage
- average effective muscle mass and time under tension

Raise the coefficient when raw INOL understates stimulus:
- longer effective ROM (for example close grip, flat back bench, high bar,
  narrow squat stance, conventional deadlift with high hips)
- more total muscle mass under meaningful tension
- higher eccentric loading or a slow, controlled descent
- a sink at the chest, or a sticking point deep in the range
- larger mechanical disadvantage at the weakest ROM point
- low volume recovery tolerance, as the same raw INOL carries more practical
  stimulus/recovery cost

Keep the coefficient closer to 1.0 when raw INOL would otherwise overstate stimulus:
- shorter effective ROM (max legal grip with a max arch, wide stance, sumo)
- strong mechanical advantage or rebound (dive bomb, bounce, wraps, stiff sleeves)
- reduced meaningful muscle mass under tension
- a lockout sticking point in a short-ROM setup
- high volume recovery tolerance, as the lifter can absorb more raw INOL for
  the same practical effect

Volume recovery tolerance is a small modifier; keep moderate neutral. Mention
which options drove the estimate and how tolerance affected it.
